To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in Next.js and WordPress, you need to configure both platforms to allow cross-origin requests. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. **WordPress:**

   a. Install and activate the "CORS" plugin from the WordPress plugin repository. This plugin will help you manage CORS settings for your website.

   b. Go to the "Settings" > "CORS" page in your WordPress dashboard.

   c. Add the following origin to the "Allowed Origins" field: `https://your-nextjs-app-domain.com`. Replace `your-nextjs-app-domain.com` with the domain name of your Next.js app.

   d. Save the changes.

2. **Next.js:**

   a. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following configuration to enable CORS:

```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        headers: [
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', value: 'true' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'X-CSRF-Token, X-Requested-With, Accept, Accept-Version, Content-Length, Content-MD5, Content-Type, Date, X-Api-Version' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: 'https://your-wordpress-site-domain.com' },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};
```

Replace `https://your-wordpress-site-domain.com` with the domain name of your WordPress site.

3. **Verify the changes:**

   a. Restart your WordPress site and Next.js app.

   b. Open your browser and navigate to `https://your-nextjs-app-domain.com/api/hello`. You should see a response from your Next.js app without any CORS

