In an MVC (Model-View-Controller) architecture, the Model and View components work together to display and interact with data in a user-friendly manner. When you add attributes to a model, it affects both the Model and View components in different ways.

1. Model Component:
   The Model component represents the application's data and business logic. It is responsible for managing the state of the application and providing methods to interact with that data. When you add attributes to a model, you are essentially adding new properties or characteristics to the data. This can affect the way the Model component stores and manages the data, as well as the methods provided to interact with it. For example, if you add a new attribute to a model representing a customer, you might need to update the Model component to store additional information about the customer, such as their address or phone number.

2. View Component:
   The View component is responsible for rendering the user interface (UI) and displaying the data from the Model component. When you add attributes to a model, the View component needs to be updated to reflect these changes. This can involve adding new UI elements to display the new attributes, or updating existing elements to reflect the changes. For example, if you add a new attribute to a model representing a customer, you might need to update the View component to display the customer's name, address, and phone number in a new layout.

