To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can introduce a simple check for distance between the newly generated planet and all existing planets before adding it to the list. If the distance is less than a certain threshold, you can generate a new planet until one with sufficient separation is found. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
function generateRandomPlanets(numPlanets, minDistance) {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
}
```

This modified function will ensure that the planets are generated at a safe distance from each other, preventing them from clumping together too closely. Adjust the `minDistance` parameter to control the desired separation between the planets.

To create a React utility to remove widows, you'll need to use the `React` and `ReactDOM` libraries. Here's a simple example of how you can achieve this:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';

const App = () => {
  const text = "Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed non risus. Suspendisse lectus tortor, dignissim sed, adipiscing nec, ultricies sed, dolor. Cras mattis consectetur purus, sed tincidunt magna aliquet non.";

  const words = text.split(" ");
  const sentences = words.reduce((acc, curr) => {
    if (curr === ".") {
      acc.push(words.slice(0, curr).join(" "));
      acc.push(words.slice(curr + 1).join(" "));
    } else {
      acc.push(words.slice(0, curr).join(" "));
    }
    return acc;
  }, []);

  const widowedSentences = sentences.filter(sentence => sentence.length <= 45);

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Widowed Sentences:</h1>
      <ul>
        {widowedSentences.map(sentence => (
          <li key={sentence}>{sentence}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

ReactDOM.render(<App />, document.getElementById('root'));
```

In this example, we first split the text into individual words using the `split()` method. We then reduce the words array into an array of sentences using the `reduce()` method. For each sentence, we check if it ends with a period. If it does, we split the sentence into two parts at the period and add them to the `acc` array. If it doesn't end with a period, we simply add the sentence to the `acc` array.

Finally, we filter the `sentences` array to only include sentences that are less than or equal to 45 words

